{
 "schema": "nmsk.synonyms.v1",
 "round": "Q298",
 "doc": "Search synonyms, abbreviations, Hebrew names and eponyms for the structures the six viewer pages ship. Q298 exists because the owner searched for the iliotibial tract as 'ITB' and as 'iliotibial band' and found nothing: both viewer engines matched the query as ONE contiguous substring of ONE field (the Z engine: the display name only; the own engine: name + id), so no abbreviation, no second name and no non-contiguous word order could ever match. This file is the data layer of the fix. Each entry names a CANONICAL structure and the other strings a clinician may type for it; `targets` are the page display names it resolves to, compared after normalisation (lower case, punctuation to spaces, runs of spaces collapsed) against BOTH the full page name and the page name with every parenthesised qualifier removed -- so 'Iliotibial (IT) tract/band', 'Tibialis anterior (continuation beyond the data-block cut, filled from Z-Anatomy)' and 'Olfactory nerve (CN I) (left)' all resolve, while 'Subtendinous bursa of tibialis anterior' does not. `re` is a regular expression over the normalised full name, for families (the Couinaud liver segments, the cranial nerves). Resolution is done at BUILD time by scripts/anatomy/q298/build_search_q298.py, per page, and recorded in data/derived/Q298_search_<key>.json, so nothing is guessed in the browser and tests/test_q298.py can assert that every abbreviation reaches a structure that really exists on at least one page.",
 "kinds": {
  "abbr": "An established published abbreviation. Only abbreviations in clinical use are listed; nothing is invented.",
  "syn": "Another name for the same structure: an English second name, a Latin / Terminologia Anatomica form, or a lay term.",
  "he": "Hebrew name. PROJECT-SUPPLIED TRANSLATION, not a published standard -- see sources.he.",
  "eponym": "A name after a person. Listed only where this project already cites the eponym (see each entry's `src`)."
 },
 "sources": {
  "gray": "Gray's Anatomy for Students, 4th ed. (Drake, Vogl, Mitchell), Elsevier 2019 -- names, second names and the abbreviations used in its text.",
  "moore": "Moore KL, Dalley AF, Agur AMR, Clinically Oriented Anatomy, 8th ed., Wolters Kluwer 2018.",
  "ta": "Terminologia Anatomica, Federative Committee on Anatomical Terminology (FICAT) 1998 -- the Latin / TA forms.",
  "stecco": "Stecco C, Functional Atlas of the Human Fascial System, Churchill Livingstone 2015 -- fascial structures and their clinical names.",
  "project": "This project's own data files, which already carry the abbreviation or the eponym: data/anatomy/abbreviations.json (99 published abbreviations, Q236), data/anatomy/structure_parts.json (the Colles / Scarpa fascial sacs and the Gerdy tubercle attachment landmark), data/derived/Q248_ligament_audit.md (the pectineal ligament of Cooper).",
  "martin1984": "Martin BF. The formation of abdomino-perineal sacs by the fasciae of Scarpa and Colles, and their clinical significance. J Anat 1984;138(Pt 4):603-616 -- cited by data/anatomy/structure_parts.json for the two fascial sacs.",
  "couinaud": "Couinaud C. Le foie: etudes anatomiques et chirurgicales. Masson, Paris 1957 -- the eight liver segments the Z pages ship as named segment meshes.",
  "he": "HEBREW IS A PROJECT-SUPPLIED TRANSLATION, NOT A PUBLISHED TERMINOLOGY. Terminologia Anatomica has no Hebrew edition. The Hebrew strings here are the terms in ordinary clinical use in Israeli medicine and physiotherapy, written for this project at the owner's request (the owner works in Hebrew). They are a search aid; the card text and every anatomical fact stay in the cited English / Latin of the sources above. Where a transliteration is what clinicians actually say (המסטרינגס, ביצפס) it is listed next to the Hebrew term."
 },
